-- 1899_ma_default_chairs_and_the_h4264_wrong_court_cluster.sql
-- The Massachusetts cohort. 74 keys written (71 blanks + 3 citation repairs), 4 blocked.
--
-- 🔴 WHY A FORWARD WRITE. Season 1 is CLOSED and IMMUTABLE. A blank is `value 0` INSERTED into
-- the OPEN season, never a DELETE -- a deleted Season 2 row falls back to Season 1 and the bad
-- chair stays visible (CC_0057). Every row written CARRIES THE SOURCES IT EXAMINED (mig 1887).
--
-- ══ MEASURED FRESH ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
-- The recorded backlog said "MA 422". Against production: 1,551 voter-visible, still-seated keys
-- cite malegislature.gov, across 197 legislators -- 232 cite ONLY a profile page (74 legislators),
-- 927 cite a bill page, 17 cite a roll call. The two defects fixed here cut across those groups,
-- so neither the old number nor any single source class is the unit. 105 MA keys sit on
-- `immigration` and cannot be written at all.
--
-- ══ DEFECT 1: THE ROW ADMITS THE CHAIR IS A DEFAULT (39 keys, 35 written) ═══════════════════
-- These rows reach a chair by conceding there is no evidence and seating the middle anyway:
--   "Not tracked by Act on Mass. No immigration legislation found in sponsored bills.
--    Moderate Democrat default for a district-focused legislator."
--   "No progressive revenue bills authored. Default current rates."
--   "Did not co-sponsor Medicare for All. No Medicare or Medicaid expansion bills found.
--    Default current program support without expansion."
-- 🔑 A CENTRE CHAIR IS A CLAIM, NOT A DEFAULT. Chair 3 is shown to voters as this person's
-- position. The corpus already has a way of saying "unknown" and this migration uses it: value 0.
-- 🔑🔑 AND A NON-SPONSORSHIP IS NOT A POSITION. 106 MA rows reason from a member NOT having
-- co-sponsored something, or from absence in a third-party tracker. An absent signature is not a
-- vote against. A YES endorses, a NO only rejects, and an unsigned line says nothing at all.
-- Only the rows that SAY "default" or "insufficient evidence" are written here -- the self-
-- condemning subset -- because that needs no judgement call. The wider 106 are recorded.
-- ⚠ One row matched the word and is NOT a defect: Sal N. DiDomenico / judicial-transparency uses
-- "a default-to-openness philosophy" in the opposite sense and cites real bills (S.1066, S.1068).
--
-- ══ DEFECT 2: THE H.4264 CLUSTER -- 39 ROWS ON A MUNICIPAL HOUSING TRUST ════════════════════
-- 39 rows across 36 legislators cite https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/H4264 as either "the
-- Massachusetts climate roadmap" or "the Environmental Justice Policy Act". H.4264 OF THE 192nd
-- IS "An Act establishing a year-round market rate rental housing trust in the town of Truro".
-- 🔑 THE NUMBER IS RIGHT FOR A DIFFERENT GENERAL COURT. H.4264 of the 191st IS "An Act relative
-- to environmental justice in the commonwealth". MA renumbers every court, exactly as Maryland
-- renumbers every session -- the same hazard that nearly produced a mass false finding in
-- migration 1898, caught there and checked for here BEFORE any verdict: every cited number was
-- resolved in the court the URL names, and then in every other court, before being called wrong.
-- The real climate roadmap is S.9 of the 192nd, "An Act creating a next-generation roadmap for
-- Massachusetts climate policy" -- which this corpus cites correctly on 21 other rows.
-- The cluster does NOT get one verdict. It gets five, and the roll call decides:
--
--   28 NOT_DISTINCTIVE   claim support for the roadmap. S.9 passed the House 144-14 and the
--                        Senate 39-1. 🔑 A near-unanimous vote cannot establish a DISTINCTIVE
--                        position -- Newhouse on 385-41 (mig 1894), Dingell on 417-10 (mig 1896).
--                        91% of the House voted the same way. BLANK.
--    3 ROLLCALL_REPAIR   Berthiaume, McKenna and Frost say they voted AGAINST it -- and they did.
--                        🔑 THE CONVERSE OF THE LEHMAN RULE: dissent from a 91% majority IS
--                        distinctive. 14 of 158. Citation repaired to S.9 + the roll call,
--                        CHAIR UNCHANGED at 4.
--    1 INVERTED_VOTE     Colleen M. Garry's row says she "voted for the 2021 climate roadmap
--                        bill". 🔴🔴 SHE VOTED NAY -- she is one of the fourteen. Retiring on the
--                        citation alone would have been right for the wrong reason; trusting it
--                        would have left an inverted chair standing. BLANK.
--    3 TRUE_BUT_LADDER   Madaro, Tyler and Holmes really ARE on the 87-name Cosponsors page of
--                        H.4264/191 (Madaro listed first, as an original petitioner). The claim
--                        is TRUE and is written into the blank so it is not lost. But the bill
--                        DIED ("No further action taken", 1/5/2021) so it was never "signed into
--                        law", and Season 2 rewrote local-environment rung 1 from "require green
--                        space, tree preservation and environmental review" to "make
--                        environmental protection the overriding priority -- block or sharply
--                        limit development". Co-sponsoring a cumulative-impact review bill is not
--                        evidence of blocking development. Carrying the chair would seat them on
--                        a position never assessed -- the Carson/campaign-finance call of mig
--                        1896. BLANK, for the ladder-scope register.
--    1 NOT_A_COSPONSOR   David Biele served in the 191st but is NOT among the 87. A checked
--                        absence, not an assumption.
--    3 NOT_SERVING       🔴🔴 Giannino (from the 192nd), Garcia and Montaño (from the 193rd) were
--                        NOT IN THE LEGISLATURE when H.4264/191 existed. The claim is not merely
--                        unsupported, it is IMPOSSIBLE. 🔑 CHECK TENURE BEFORE READING A
--                        SPONSORSHIP OR A NON-VOTE -- the rule the Texas SB 17 pass paid for.
--
-- ⚠ THE ROLL-CALL PARSE IS ASYMMETRIC AND THE BLANKS SAY SO. The PDF is a four-column grid that
-- pdftotext splits mid-cell, so continuation names lose their marker. The 14 NAYS parse cleanly
-- and were verified against the roll call's OWN declared total of 14 before any name was used.
-- The YEA parse recovers only 39 of 144, so NOTHING is asserted from it: this migration proves
-- that someone voted Nay, never that someone voted Yea.
--
-- ══ BLOCKED: `immigration`, FOR THE FOURTH MIGRATION RUNNING ════════════════════════════════
-- 4 of these keys cannot be written because `immigration` has NO Season 2 pin. Added to migs
-- 1896 (3), 1897 (3) and 1898 (20), **30 defective chairs now stand unfixable on that one
-- topic**, and 105 MA keys in total sit on it. A pre-guard fails loudly if it ever gains a pin.
--
-- ⚠ NOT FIXED HERE, DELIBERATELY: a sweep of all 676 distinct bills this cohort cites -- "does
-- the cited bill say what the row says it says?" -- was still running when this migration was
-- cut. The H.4264 cluster is one bill out of 676, found because it was the most-cited; the sweep
-- is the way to find the rest and is recorded as the next step. Also untouched: the 232
-- profile-only keys, which need the per-court sponsored/cosponsored lists the way migration 1898
-- read Maryland's.

