islature.gov/Bills/192/H4264', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/C_T1']::text[]),
  -- Colleen M. Garry / climate-change  (Season 1 chair 3 -> 0, INVERTED_VOTE)
  ('c8f917f9-f6c4-49f8-b5a4-c330b07f90d2'::uuid, 'f1e44d66-5d27-4b51-b54f-b7ace86f6a3c'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '5f1403f3-90b6-491f-ba54-3c8e46a5ae26'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴🔴 THE ROW STATES THE OPPOSITE OF THE RECORD. It says "Garry voted for the 2021 climate roadmap bill overall". Garry voted NAY. The Clerk''s roll call -- Massachusetts House, Yea and Nay No. 2, 28 January 2021, S.9 on engrossment, 144 YEAS to 14 NAYS -- names the fourteen dissenters, and Garry is among them (parse verified against the roll call''s own declared total of 14). The 2021 climate roadmap is S.9 of the 192nd General Court, "An Act creating a next-generation roadmap for Massachusetts climate policy". H.4264 of the 192nd -- the bill this row actually cites -- is "An Act establishing a year-round market rate rental housing trust in the town of Truro". 🔑 Retiring this row on the citation alone would have been right for the wrong reason, and trusting the citation would have left an INVERTED chair standing. Only the primary record separates the two. The vote is verified and is genuinely distinctive -- a Nay against a 91% majority -- but the chair here was seated on the belief that the member voted the other way, so there is no chair to keep; re-seating on the true vote is new research, not a repair. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/H4264', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S9', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/192/HouseRollCall2.pdf', 'https://web.archive.org/web/20260310193641/https://actonmass.org/legislators/colleen-garry/']::text[]),
  -- Daniel M. Donahue / climate-change  (Season 1 chair 2 -> 0, NOT_DISTINCTIVE)
  ('71aa11da-0fa1-49d2-bcbd-3bce7d0bad2b'::uuid, 'f1e44d66-5d27-4b51-b54f-b7ace86f6a3c'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '5f1403f3-90b6-491f-ba54-3c8e46a5ae26'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴🔴 THE CITED BILL IS A MUNICIPAL HOUSING TRUST, NOT THE CLIMATE ROADMAP. The 2021 climate roadmap is S.9 of the 192nd General Court, "An Act creating a next-generation roadmap for Massachusetts climate policy". H.4264 of the 192nd -- the bill this row actually cites -- is "An Act establishing a year-round market rate rental housing trust in the town of Truro". The number is right for a different General Court: H.4264 of the 191st is "An Act relative to environmental justice in the commonwealth" -- but that bill DIED, "No further action taken" on 1/5/2021, and its sponsor is the Joint Committee on Environment, Natural Resources and Agriculture, not any individual member. 🔴 AND THE REAL INSTRUMENT CANNOT SEAT THIS CHAIR EITHER. S.9 passed the House 144 YEAS to 14 NAYS (Roll Call No. 2, 28 January 2021, verified against the roll call''s own declared totals) and the Senate 39 to 1. 🔑 A near-unanimous vote cannot establish a DISTINCTIVE position -- the rule that blanked Newhouse on 385-41 in migration 1894 and Dingell on 417-10 in 1896. 91% of the House voted the same way, so a Yea distinguishes this member from almost nobody. ⚠ THE CONVERSE DOES NOT HOLD, and three rows in this same cluster were REPAIRED rather than blanked for exactly that reason: dissenting from a 91% majority IS distinctive, and the 14 Nays are named in the roll call. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/DMD1/Bills', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/H4264']::text[]),
