  ('799c1cea-4020-4c50-b7d1-1e9aac867529'::uuid, 'f1e44d66-5d27-4b51-b54f-b7ace86f6a3c'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, '5f1403f3-90b6-491f-ba54-3c8e46a5ae26'::uuid, 'Citation repaired 2026-10-07 (migration 1899); THE CHAIR IS UNCHANGED. The claim was right and the citation was wrong. This row said Frost "voted against the Massachusetts climate roadmap legislation (H.4264, 2021)". The vote is VERIFIED: Massachusetts House, Yea and Nay No. 2, 28 January 2021, S.9 on engrossment, 144 YEAS to 14 NAYS, and Frost is one of the fourteen Nays (the parse was checked against the roll call''s own declared total of 14 before any name was read off it). The 2021 climate roadmap is S.9 of the 192nd General Court, "An Act creating a next-generation roadmap for Massachusetts climate policy". H.4264 of the 192nd -- the bill this row actually cites -- is "An Act establishing a year-round market rate rental housing trust in the town of Truro". 🔑 A BAD CITATION IS NOT A FALSE CLAIM. 🔑 And a near-unanimous vote cannot seat a chair for the MAJORITY, but dissent from it is distinctive: 14 of 158. ⚠ The chair is carried across seasons only after comparing the rung''s OWN TEXT at this value: Season 1 rung 4 read "let market forces drive any transition to cleaner energy sources" and Season 2 rung 4 reads "Stay neutral on energy and let the market choose among all sources" -- the same position in different words, so the chair does not move.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/S9', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/PKF1', 'https://malegislature.gov/RollCall/192/HouseRollCall2.pdf']::text[]),
