  -- Russell E. Holmes / local-environment  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 0, TRUE_BUT_LADDER_SCOPE)
  ('e96b35eb-dad1-4a29-b416-d9b10838840f'::uuid, '1935979c-b290-42e4-baa5-8cb0138b4ffa'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'd67eabf7-8da0-4ca7-b2af-74745b3bfd47'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07, and ⚠ THE UNDERLYING CLAIM IS TRUE -- it is recorded here so it is not lost. Holmes IS on the Cosponsors page of H.4264 of the 191st General Court, "An Act relative to environmental justice in the commonwealth" (Adrian C. Madaro is listed first, as an original petitioner). TWO THINGS ARE STILL WRONG. 🔴 THE CITATION POINTS AT THE WRONG BILL: the row cites H.4264 of the 192nd, which is "An Act establishing a year-round market rate rental housing trust in the town of Truro". And the bill that IS meant died -- "No further action taken", 1/5/2021 -- so it was never "signed into law in 2021" as this row states; the environmental justice provisions reached the statute book inside S.9, the climate roadmap. 🔴🔴 AND THE SEASON 2 LADDER ASKS SOMETHING STRONGER THAN THE EVIDENCE SUPPORTS. Season 1 rung 1 read "Require significant green space, tree preservation, and environmental review before approving any development"; Season 2 rung 1 reads "Make environmental protection the overriding priority -- block or sharply limit development that would harm the local environment". Co-sponsoring a cumulative-impact review bill for environmental justice communities is not evidence of blocking development, so carrying the chair across would seat this member on a position never assessed. That is the Carson/campaign-finance call from migration 1896, and it belongs to the ladder-scope register. A verified fact with nowhere to sit is still a verified fact: it is written here so the next researcher starts from it rather than from nothing. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/191/H4264/Cosponsor', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/H4264', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/REH1']::text[]),
  -- Samantha Montaño / local-environment  (Season 1 chair 1 -> 0, NOT_SERVING)
  ('28a703f8-8316-4d3c-bfc0-3dcd77eab96c'::uuid, '1935979c-b290-42e4-baa5-8cb0138b4ffa'::uuid, '86d893a1-c1a2-4bbf-b4e5-69ec43221194'::uuid, 'd67eabf7-8da0-4ca7-b2af-74745b3bfd47'::uuid, 'Blanked 2026-10-07. 🔴🔴 THE MEMBER WAS NOT IN THE LEGISLATURE WHEN THE CLAIMED BILL EXISTED. This row says Montaño co-sponsored the Environmental Justice Policy Act, H.4264. The environmental justice bill of that number is H.4264 of the 191st General Court (2019-2020); the bill the row actually cites, H.4264 of the 192nd, is "An Act establishing a year-round market rate rental housing trust in the town of Truro". Montaño''s own malegislature profile lists service beginning with the 193rd General Court, so the member could not have co-sponsored a bill of the 191st at all. The claim is not merely unsupported, it is IMPOSSIBLE. 🔑 CHECK TENURE BEFORE READING A SPONSORSHIP OR A NON-VOTE -- the rule from the Texas SB 17 pass, which cost two wrong blanks there. The sources examined are kept below so the next reader does not re-derive this. This is a blank, not a deletion -- the chair may well be re-researchable, and that is an invitation.', ARRAY['https://malegislature.gov/Bills/191/H4264', 'https://malegislature.gov/Bills/192/H4264', 'https://malegislature.gov/Legislators/Profile/S_M1']::text[]),
